# PocketClaw production signing certificate — SHA-256 fingerprint.
#
# PUBLIC METADATA. This file holds one thing: the SHA-256 digest of the
# production signing *certificate*. A certificate is the public half. It is not
# a key, it carries no password, and publishing it is normal — Google Play shows
# it, and anyone can read it out of a signed APK with apksigner. Committing it
# is the point: it is what lets the release gate prove that an artifact was
# signed by the key this project intends to ship with, and not by some other key
# that also happens to verify.
#
# NEVER put the keystore, an alias password, or any key material in this file or
# anywhere else in this repository. See docs/RELEASE_SIGNING.md.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATUS: ENROLLED
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The developer app-signing key was created by the owner at the H2 ceremony on
# 2026-09-10. The keystore and both passwords are owner-held and live outside
# this repository; nothing but the digest below is, or ever will be, committed.
#
# One digest, and only one. A second enrolled signer would mean the gate
# accepted either of two identities without saying which, which is the opposite
# of what enrolling a fingerprint is for. Rotating the key means replacing this
# line, not adding another.
#
# The certificate subject is deliberately not a trust input. Subjects are
# self-asserted and anyone can mint a certificate claiming one; the digest is
# what actually identifies the key.
#
# Re-read it from the keystore at any time — this prints exactly the form below,
# and asks for the keystore password, which it never stores:
#
#     tool/create_release_keystore.sh --print-fingerprint <keystore> <alias>
#
# or from a signed artifact:
#
#     apksigner verify --print-certs <apk> | grep 'SHA-256 digest'
#
